  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry weather will prevail through Tuesday night.\n\nHigh pressure will settle south into the Mid Atlantic states tonight \nbefore drifting off the east coast by Tuesday night. A surface ridge \nextending back into PA and Western NY will maintain dry weather for \nmost of the area through Tuesday night. A sharpening mid level trough \ncrossing the Canadian Maritimes will push a backdoor cold front \nsouth across northern NY and New England, but any showers associated \nwith this front should remain north and east of Jefferson and Lewis \ncounties late tonight through Tuesday morning.\n\nRiver valley fog will develop once again across the Southern Tier \nand Finger Lakes late tonight and persist through mid morning \nTuesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A cold front will cross the eastern Great Lakes \nWednesday through Wednesday night, producing showers and \nthunderstorms.\n\nA mid level trough will move from far northwest Ontario Wednesday to \nnorthern Quebec by Thursday, with the southern extent of height \nfalls and a belt of enhanced low/mid level flow spreading east \nacross the Great Lakes. Large scale forcing for ascent will arrive \nlater Wednesday and Wednesday evening ahead of the trough, with low \nlevel convergence becoming enhanced along a pre-frontal trough and \nlake breeze boundaries Wednesday afternoon and evening. A narrow \nplume of rich moisture will be captured and advected northeast into \nour region just ahead of the system, with surface dewpoints rising \ninto the 65-70F range and PWATs briefly exceeding 2 inches. \n\nThe quality of moisture and adequate dynamics will support an area \nof showers and scattered thunderstorms crossing the region from \nnorthwest to southeast, peaking in coverage late Wednesday afternoon \nand evening. The high PWAT environment will support torrential \ndownpours in any storms, although the progressive nature of the \nsystem should keep the flood risk relatively low. \n\nModerate deep layer shear will overspread the area Wednesday, but \ninstability is forecast to remain weak with very warm mid levels and \npoor low/mid level lapse rates. The lack of stronger instability \nwill likely hold down the severe weather risk.",
